    {
      "id": "rn_YXZBtqvvgTHppc4Q::L2::Q8",
      "moduleId": "rn_YXZBtqvvgTHppc4Q::L2",
      "stem": "Explain why the spin direction is represented by arrows in box notation and why paired arrows must point oppositely.",
      "misconceptionClass": "arrow semantics",
      "kind": "explain-why",
      "modelAnswer": "Electron spin behaves like rotation about its own axis, creating a tiny magnetic moment; box diagrams draw that moment as an arrow, and two electrons sharing one orbital must differ in spin direction, so their arrows run up and down rather than both the same way.",
